Article 2 Any person who fabricates reasons for exiting the country, personal identity information, or relevant certificates of overseas relations for the purpose of organizing others to illegally cross the national border (frontier) shall be determined as "practicing fraud" as provided for in the first paragraph of Article 319 of the Criminal Law.
"Exit documents" as provided for in the first paragraph of Article 319 of the Criminal Law includes passports or international travel documents used in lieu of passports, the Seaman's Book of the People's Republic of China, the Exit-Entry Permit of the People's Republic of China, the Travel Certificate of the People's Republic of China, documents for travel of Chinese citizens to and from Hong Kong, Macao, and Taiwan, the border area exit-entry pass, visas, endorsements, exit (entry) certificates, name lists, and other materials required to be inspected upon exiting the country.
Any of the following circumstances shall be determined as "serious circumstances" as provided for in the first paragraph of Article 319 of the Criminal Law:
(1) Obtaining five or more exit documents by fraud;
(2) Illegally collecting fees of 300,000 RMB or more;
(3) Knowingly obtaining exit documents by fraud for a person who is prohibited from exiting the country under state regulations;
(4) Other circumstances of a serious nature.

Article 3 "Exit-entry documents" as provided for in Article 320 of the Criminal Law includes the documents listed in paragraph 2 of Article 2 of this Interpretation and other materials required to be inspected upon entering the country.
Any of the following circumstances shall be determined as "serious circumstances" as provided for in Article 320 of the Criminal Law:
(1) Providing forged or altered exit-entry documents to others or selling five or more exit-entry documents;
(2) Illegally collecting fees of 300,000 RMB or more;
(3) Knowingly providing forged or altered exit-entry documents to a person who is prohibited from exiting or entering the country under state regulations or selling exit-entry documents to such a person;
(4) Other circumstances of a serious nature.

Article 5 Any of the following circumstances in illegally crossing the national border (frontier) shall be determined as "serious circumstances" as provided for in Article 322 of the Criminal Law:
(1) Engaging in acts that harm national interests outside the country;
(2) Illegally crossing the national border (frontier) three times or more, or in a group of three or more persons;
(3) Soliciting or enticing others to illegally cross the national border (frontier) together;
(4) Colluding with overseas organizations or persons to illegally cross the national border (frontier);
(5) Illegally crossing the national border (frontier) again within one year after receiving an administrative penalty for illegally crossing the national border (frontier);
(6) Other circumstances of a serious nature.

Article 6 Any of the following circumstances shall be determined as the act of "illegally crossing the national border (frontier)" as provided for in Section 3 of Chapter VI of the Criminal Law:
(1) Entering or exiting the national border (frontier) without exit-entry documents or evading border inspection;
(2) Using forged, altered, or invalid exit-entry documents to enter or exit the national border (frontier);
(3) Using another person's exit-entry documents to enter or exit the national border (frontier);
(4) Using exit-entry documents obtained by fraud through falsely stated reasons for entry or exit, concealing true identity, or fraudulently using another person's identity documents to enter or exit the national border (frontier);
(5) Illegally entering or exiting the national border (frontier) by other means.
